over 4 days and cited national security as the motivation. This time, BGP or Routing Announcements were not used to implement the shutdown. IODA measurement data shows that BGP was largely un-impacted. Instead, the government used various techniques to shut off Iran’s connectivity with the global Internet, while allowing specific, sanctioned access via the NIN in a policy called whitelisting. Organizations that support digital human rights in Iran report that Iranians were able to circumvent the shutdown using various VPNs and censorship resilient technologies built for autocratic, closed environment contexts
