> A few tools can still help people in Iran stay connected, but none of them are very stable at the moment.
> 
> Things like `DNSTT` and `Splitstream / Slipnet` sometimes work on certain ISPs, but the reliability is pretty inconsistent.
> The domains used by these tools tend to get detected and blocked fairly quickly, sometimes within a short time. On top of that, latency is usually quite high, which makes the connection unstable and not very practical for tasks like uploading large files or videos.
> 
> Telegram proxies are also widely used and can be very helpful for basic communication. Many people rely on them to stay connected to news and channels. However, they run into similar issues, latency can be high and speeds often fluctuate. Sometimes the connection becomes so slow that even loading simple messages can take a while.
> 
> Another big challenge is DNS. Finding clean, usable DNS servers has become very difficult. Many public DNS addresses are either blocked or heavily interfered with. Because of this, people often have to scan large IP ranges to discover working DNS servers, and that process can be quite slow and time-consuming.
> 
> So while these methods can help temporarily, keeping them working requires constant effort, new domains, new proxy servers, and continuous testing because blocks tend to happen quickly once something starts getting widely used.
